  Manifest for the Microdroid payload APK.

  This is not an application. It is a container whose only purpose is to carry a
  signed, fs-verity covered bundle of (native payload library + VM
  configuration) to a protected VM. So the manifest declares:

    * no launcher activity, so nothing on the device can start it as an app
    * no exported components, so nothing can bind to it
    * no uses-permission, so it holds nothing to revoke
    * android:hasCode="false", because there is no dex; the code is in
      jni/arm64-v8a/libxvault_payload.so

  The package name still matters: it is part of what the APK signature and the
  .idsig digest commit to, and therefore part of the VM identity pvmfw derives.
  Changing it changes the VM's secret.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.xrom.xvault">

    <application
        android:label="X-Vault Microdroid Payload"
        android:hasCode="false" />

</manifest>
